#+TITLE: vpasswd-postgres-convert — DSMR Migration Documentation
#+AUTHOR: Brian O'Reilly <fade@deepsky.com>
#+DATE: 2026-04-13

* Summary

~vpasswd-postgres-convert.sh~ migrates legacy vpasswd/vpasswd.cdb file-based
vpopmail authentication to the PostgreSQL backend used by the DSMR stack.

Older vpopmail installations used flat files (~vpasswd~ and ~vpasswd.cdb~) in
each domain directory for user authentication.  The DSMR vpopmail package uses
PostgreSQL as the authentication backend.  When upgrading a system that still
has legacy vpasswd files, those users must be imported into the PostgreSQL
~vpopmail~ table before authentication will work.

| Field     | Value                                             |
|-----------+---------------------------------------------------|
| Script    | ~/home/vpopmail/bin/vpasswd-postgres-convert.sh~  |
| Package   | vpopmail                                          |
| Requires  | root or vpopmail user, running PostgreSQL, ~psql~ |

* Automatic Detection on Upgrade

When the vpopmail package is installed or upgraded, the postinst script checks
for legacy vpasswd files.  If any are found, it runs the converter in dry-run
mode and prints a summary.  You must then run the script manually with
~--commit~ to apply the migration.  This ensures you can review the dry-run
output before any data is modified.

* Usage

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
# Dry-run: scan all domains, report what would change (no modifications)
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vpasswd-postgres-convert.sh

# Dry-run a specific domain only
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vpasswd-postgres-convert.sh --domain example.com

# Apply changes: insert/update users in PostgreSQL, rename vpasswd files
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vpasswd-postgres-convert.sh --commit

# Apply changes to a specific domain
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vpasswd-postgres-convert.sh --commit --domain example.com
#+END_SRC

The ~--domain~ flag can be repeated to process multiple specific domains.

* What It Does

For each domain directory under ~/home/vpopmail/domains/~:

1. *Checks for a vpasswd file.*  Domains without one are already using
   database authentication and are skipped.

2. *Parses each vpasswd entry.*  The file format is colon-delimited:
   ~pw_name:pw_passwd:pw_uid:pw_gid:pw_gecos:pw_dir:pw_shell~

3. *Compares against PostgreSQL.*  For each user:
   - *Missing from DB*: inserted with all fields from vpasswd.
   - *Present and matching*: reported as OK, no action taken.
   - *Present but mismatched*: the specific differing fields are reported
     and (with ~--commit~) updated to match the vpasswd file.

4. *Retires the flat files* (with ~--commit~): ~vpasswd~ and ~vpasswd.cdb~ are
   renamed to timestamped ~.bak~ files (e.g. ~vpasswd.bak.20260413112429~).
   The ~.vpasswd.lock~ file is removed.

* vpasswd File Format Reference

Each line in a vpasswd file corresponds to one virtual user:

#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
username:encrypted_password:uid:gid:gecos:home_directory:quota
#+END_EXAMPLE

| Field    | Example                                    | DB column   |
|----------+--------------------------------------------+-------------|
| username | ~postmaster~                               | ~pw_name~   |
| password | ~$6$salt$hash...~                          | ~pw_passwd~ |
| uid      | ~0~                                        | ~pw_uid~    |
| gid      | ~0~                                        | ~pw_gid~    |
| gecos    | ~Postmaster~                               | ~pw_gecos~  |
| home_dir | ~/home/vpopmail/domains/ex.com/postmaster~ | ~pw_dir~    |
| quota    | ~NOQUOTA~ or ~50000000S~                   | ~pw_shell~  |

* Rollback

The original vpasswd files are preserved as timestamped ~.bak~ files in each
domain directory.

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
# Restore the flat file
cd /home/vpopmail/domains/example.com
mv vpasswd.bak.20260413112429 vpasswd

# Remove inserted rows from PostgreSQL
psql -U vpopmail -d vpopmail -c \
  "DELETE FROM vpopmail WHERE pw_domain = 'example.com'"
#+END_SRC
